<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Hot / cold storage tier — EXAMPLE, not loaded by default.

  Declares a second disk (`cold`, e.g. a large, slow HDD mounted into the
  container at /var/lib/clickhouse-cold/) and a storage policy `hot_cold` whose
  volumes are, in order:

    default — the hot volume: the existing `default` disk (/var/lib/clickhouse/, fast)
    cold    — the `cold` disk

  The hot volume MUST be named `default`: ClickHouse only lets an existing table
  switch to a policy that contains every volume of its current policy, which is
  `default` with a single volume `default`. A volume named `hot` is refused with
  "New storage policy `hot_cold` shall contain volumes of the old storage policy
  `default`" (verified on 24.3).

  New parts are always written to the first volume (hot). AS-Stats moves older
  data to the cold volume itself, per table, from the "Move to cold after
  (days)" value set in Admin › Storage (retention_policies.cold_after_days):
  the reconciler adds a `TTL … TO VOLUME 'cold'` clause and moves the
  partitions that predate it with `ALTER TABLE … MOVE PARTITION … TO VOLUME
  'cold'`. It only does so for tables whose storage policy has a volume named
  exactly `cold` — keep that name.

  To use it:
    1. copy this file to storage-hot-cold.xml and bind-mount the FILE (not the
       directory) at /etc/clickhouse-server/config.d/storage-hot-cold.xml;
    2. bind-mount the second disk at /var/lib/clickhouse-cold (owned by the
       clickhouse user, uid 101 in the official image);
    3. restart ClickHouse, then once per table:
         ALTER TABLE asstats.flows_log       MODIFY SETTING storage_policy = 'hot_cold';
         ALTER TABLE asstats.traffic_by_port MODIFY SETTING storage_policy = 'hot_cold';
  See README › "Cold storage on a second disk".

  prefer_not_to_merge on the cold volume keeps ClickHouse from re-merging (and
  so re-writing) parts that already sit on the slow disk. The parts moved there
  are closed, fully merged partitions, so nothing is lost; whole partitions
  past their retention are dropped by the reconciler's partition sweep, which
  does not need a merge.

  move_factor is 0: parts move only by age (the reconciler / TTL), never
  because the hot disk is filling up. ClickHouse's default (0.1) moves parts
  chosen by size, not age, once the hot volume is under 10 % free, which can
  put the newest data on the slow disk. A full hot disk is what the
  disk_usage alert rule is for.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <storage_configuration>
        <disks>
            <!-- `default` (/var/lib/clickhouse/) is implicit; only the new disk is declared. -->
            <cold>
                <path>/var/lib/clickhouse-cold/</path>
            </cold>
        </disks>
        <policies>
            <hot_cold>
                <volumes>
                    <default>
                        <disk>default</disk>
                    </default>
                    <cold>
                        <disk>cold</disk>
                        <prefer_not_to_merge>true</prefer_not_to_merge>
                    </cold>
                </volumes>
                <move_factor>0</move_factor>
            </hot_cold>
        </policies>
    </storage_configuration>
</clickhouse>
